# Nested calls that all fall back are checked in bounded time. Every call here is first tried with
# the expected type, which fails at `xs`, and then on its own; each retry would check the nested
# call again, so without a bound the work doubles at every level. A variable that cannot take the
# hint is seen before anything else is checked, and a failing argument that is not a variable
# (`copy(xs)`) spends the budget of retries. Neither takes the expected type from a call that
# comes later, in another statement or as another argument of the same call.
fn choose_nested[T](inner: Int?, xs: Array[T], fallback: T) -> T:
    fallback

fn pick[T](a: T, b: T) -> T:
    a

